preConditionHook:
// BROOKS'S STANDARD TRADING-RANGE TARGET: a breakout out of a range tends to
// run about the range's own HEIGHT beyond it, so a long is closed when the high
// reaches the top of the rangeLookback-bar range plus that range's height, and a
// short when the low reaches the bottom less it.
//
// THE ROLLING FORM IS DELIBERATE and is the merge pass's decision, taken on the
// brief's most-mechanizable rule and proposed by R08-13's own notes: the window
// ends ONE BAR AGO and is re-read every bar, so the projection DRIFTS as the
// window rolls. That is the cost, and it is the whole distinction from
// PatternHeightMeasuredMoveTarget, which captures Highest(High[1], refBars) and
// Lowest(Low[1], refBars) ONCE at entry and never moves the level again. The
// two therefore fire on different bars on every trade that lasts more than one
// bar, and neither is the other at any parameter -- the same frozen/rolling
// distinction that keeps RollingRangeMidpointTarget apart from
// GapMidpointMeasuredMoveTarget, and the same reason OpeningRangeMeasuredMoveTarget
// is kept apart from PatternHeightMeasuredMoveTarget. Brooks's own caveat is
// that the distance is approximate however it is measured.
//
// Not RangeMidpointTarget, which exits AT the midpoint of the window -- inside
// the range rather than a full height beyond its edge. Not AtrProfitTarget,
// which scales OPEN PROFIT by ATR and never looks at where the range was: this
// projects a PRICE from the window's own height, so where the position was
// entered does not enter into it. Not ExitAtPriorRangeExtreme, whose target is
// the window's far edge itself with no projection beyond it.
//
// THE WINDOW ENDS ONE BAR AGO -- Highest(High, rangeLookback)[1] -- so the bar
// being tested cannot raise the target it is being tested against; without that
// offset a bar making a new high would push its own target up by two ticks for
// every one it gained and the rule could never fire. The scans mirror
// EasyLanguage's Extremes(), which Highest() and Lowest() call: seeded at the
// window's first bar, scanning the rest, strict comparison, EL SOURCE and so
// carrying the relational tolerance -- el_gt / el_lt, not raw > and <.
//
// The MarketPosition guard is what makes it an Exit: the projection is computed
// every bar whether or not a position is open.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage. Read once into a local.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
rangeHigh = high[1];
rangeLow = low[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)rangeLookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], rangeHigh)) rangeHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], rangeLow)) rangeLow = low[i];
}
tgtUp = rangeHigh + (rangeHigh - rangeLow);
tgtDn = rangeLow - (rangeHigh - rangeLow);
